# Slice D0 (Phase E1, 2026-04-29) — line-ending policy for launcher scripts.
#
# Without explicit rules, `core.autocrlf=true` (the default on most
# Windows installs) flips LF to CRLF on checkout for *every* text file.
# That's catastrophic for shell scripts: bash refuses to run a CRLF
# script with `bad interpreter` errors, breaking the macOS / Linux
# launchers (`harness-start.sh`, `install-version.sh`, `check-update.sh`).
#
# We pin the convention per-file-type so it survives any cloner's
# personal `autocrlf` setting:

# Default: autodetect text files, normalize internally to LF, leave
# working-tree EOL up to platform.
* text=auto

# Windows scripts: must be CRLF on disk for cmd.exe / PowerShell.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf

# Unix scripts: must be LF on disk for bash / sh / zsh.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Source files: stay neutral (autocrlf=true cloners get CRLF, Linux
# users get LF, internal storage is LF either way).
*.js text
*.mjs text
*.cjs text
*.json text
*.md text
*.yml text
*.yaml text
*.html text
*.css text

# Binary assets: never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
